There is no mathematics on this page: no formulas, derivations or computations. It is part of the fair copy of an official expert report (Gutachten) addressed to the Hannoverian university Curatorium. The Göttingen professors, Riemann presumably among the signatories, are responding to the Prussian government's invitation (Baeyer's plan of 1861, 'Über die Größe und Figur der Erde') to join the Central European Arc Measurement (Mitteleuropäische Gradmessung).

Content of the page, continuing the previous one:
(1) The signatories say that, after mature consideration, they found some more specific discussions necessary. They submit these separately in an attachment (Anlage) to the report, so that they can refer to the motives developed there later on.
(2) They summarise the Curatorium's own reading of the files of the Hannoverian arc measurement of 1820–1848. Those files emphasise what Gauss had already done, or what had been done with his participation, to connect the Hannoverian triangulations with those of other countries, serving the higher scientific aim of an exact knowledge of the figure of the Earth. The connections listed are:
  1) the Danish triangulations carried out by Schumacher;
  2) the French-English triangulations, linked through the Dutch triangulation of Krayenhoff and rectified by new measurements in East Frisia, Oldenburg, etc.;
  3) the Prussian triangulations of Müffling and others;
  4) the Hessian triangulations of Gerling, and through these (continued on the next page) the Darmstadt, Bavarian, Württemberg and Austrian ones.

The argument of the report, completed on the next page, is that the Hannoverian triangulation is complete and already connected to its neighbours. The new project therefore should not require a resumption of triangulation work.

There is nothing to restate in modern notation.
